<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">British Raj</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Raj, Indian Raj</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The British Raj was the system of direct British crown rule over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a> from 1858 to 1947, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a>'s rule ended following the rebellion of 1857. It was headed by a viceroy and administered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-civil-service">Indian Civil Service</a>. The Raj was the centrepiece of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>'s independence in 1947 marked the beginning of its dissolution.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/british-raj.jpg" alt="British Raj" width="320" height="261" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Edinburgh Geographical Institute; J. G. Bartholomew and Sons. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:British_Indian_Empire_1909_Imperial_Gazetteer_of_India.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Australia (1901), New Zealand and South Africa (1910) — which had elected parliaments and substantial autonomy — to directly administered Crown Colonies, the <b>Indian Raj</b> governed by a viceroy and the Indian Civil Service, and the protectorates and League of Nations mandates administered under varying degrees of oversight.</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-empire">British Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>British Raj</b> was born from the crisis of the Indian Rebellion of 1857.</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-raj">British Raj · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Its integration of diverse religious and ethnic groups under a centralised state provided a model of imperial rule that informed successor regimes in the region, including the <b>British Raj</b>, which adopted elements of Mughal revenue administration.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>By the 1880s Britain ruled India through the <b>British Raj</b>, established when the Crown took over the government of India from the East India Company after the Indian Rebellion of 1857.</blockquote><a href="/movement/indian-independence-movement">Indian independence movement · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>…in The Dual Mandate in British Tropical Africa (1922), existing rulers such as the emirs of the Sokoto Caliphate in Northern Nigeria kept their courts and taxes under British supervision, as the Indian princely states did under the <b>Raj</b>; where no suitable ruler existed, administrators appointed “warrant chiefs”.</blockquote><a href="/government/colonial-rule">Colonial rule · How It Works</a></li><li><blockquote>Most Indians were peasants, who paid the land tax on which the <b>Raj</b>’s finances rested and died in the tens of millions in the famines of the later 19th century; the communities treated as untouchable stood outside the caste order altogether, and more than 1.6 million Indians were…</blockquote><a href="/societies/british-raj">Social Hierarchy of British India</a></li></ul></section>
